THINK = {
1: r"Mathematical language is just English with the ambiguity removed. When you read a symbolic statement, say it aloud in plain words; when you meet an English claim, ask ``for \emph{every} what? for \emph{some} what?'' Almost every proof mistake starts with a quantifier that was quietly misread.",
2: r"A proof is a conversation with a sceptical friend who accepts only what you can justify. Do not start by writing; start by asking ``what do I know, and what do I want?'' and by trying a small example to see \emph{why} the claim is true. The proof is then you writing that reason down so clearly that the friend has nothing left to object to.",
3: r"Induction works like a line of dominoes: if the first falls, and each one knocks over the next, they all fall. The skill is finding what ``the next one'' means. Ask: \emph{if I already knew the claim for $n$, what extra step would give me $n+1$?} If you can answer that, the proof nearly writes itself.",
4: r"Sets and functions are the nouns and verbs of the whole subject. Be patient with the definitions: they feel fussy, but each one is a precise answer to a natural question (``does every output get hit?'' is surjective; ``are any two inputs sent to the same output?'' is the opposite of injective). When a definition confuses you, test it on a tiny set you can write out in full.",
5: r"Numbers hide structure: every integer is built from primes, and the gcd is the shared part of two numbers. The Euclidean algorithm works because the common divisors of $a$ and $b$ do not change when you replace $a$ by its remainder; once you see \emph{why} nothing is lost, the algorithm stops being a recipe and becomes obvious.",
6: r"Modular arithmetic means we only care about the remainder, like reading a clock. That is a huge simplification: infinitely many numbers collapse to $m$ classes. The way of thinking is to ask, for any problem about divisibility or last digits, ``can I replace each number by its remainder and work in the small world instead?''",
7: r"The Chinese remainder theorem says that knowing a number's remainders modulo several coprime numbers is the same as knowing it modulo their product. Think of it as describing a place by two separate coordinates. Fermat and Euler say that powers eventually repeat in a regular way, which is why enormous exponents can be shrunk before you compute anything.",
8: r"Counting is about structure, not formulas. Before reaching for $\binom{n}{k}$, ask: ``what are the choices, in what order do I make them, and do different choice-paths ever produce the same object?'' If you can describe how to \emph{build} each object exactly once, you have the count.",
9: r"When you count objects with ``at least one'' of several properties, directly listing them double-counts. Inclusion--exclusion corrects this like a careful accountant: add everything, subtract overlaps, add back what was subtracted too often. A generating function is a different idea: a clothesline that carries the whole sequence of answers at once, so you can combine counting problems by multiplying.",
10: r"A recurrence tells you how to get the next term from earlier ones, which is exactly how you would explain the pattern to a friend. The goal is to turn that step-by-step description into a direct formula. Guess from small cases, then \emph{prove} the guess by induction; do not trust a pattern until it is proved.",
11: r"A graph strips a situation down to dots and lines, and the theorems here are about what that skeleton forces. Handshake counting shows how much a single trick (count the same thing two ways) can do. When you meet a graph proof, ask what is being counted, and whether you can count it once from each side.",
12: r"Probability on finite spaces is counting in disguise: list the equally likely outcomes, count those that match. The most important habit is to be explicit about the sample space before computing anything, because most famous paradoxes come from quietly changing what the outcomes are.",
13: r"Almost every inequality begins with ``a square cannot be negative.'' Look for places where you can create a square, or where two quantities are being compared by a hidden balance. When an inequality is tight (equal) for some values, those values tell you which technique to try.",
14: r"A functional equation is a puzzle in which the unknown is an entire function. Start by playing: plug in $0$, $1$, equal values, and see what the equation \emph{forces}. Then decide what kind of function it must be and finally check that your candidate truly works; many attempts lose points by skipping that last check.",
15: r"Olympiad geometry rewards looking for the \emph{reason} an angle or a length is what it is. Draw a large, careful picture, mark every equal angle you can justify, and look for circles hiding in the figure. If you are stuck, you can always switch to coordinates, a slower but reliable backup.",
16: r"When you are asked whether something is possible, look for something that never changes (an invariant) or that only moves one way (a monovariant). If a target state breaks the unchanging quantity, it is impossible; if a quantity must keep shrinking, the process must stop. Try small cases until a quantity suggests itself.",
17: r"A proof is only worth the points a reader can find in it. Under time pressure, the way of thinking is to be strategic: secure the problems you can finish, write each as if for a stranger, and never leave a half-claim that you have not marked as unproved. Honest partial progress earns credit; a bluff costs it.",
}
